\begindata{lset,44980859136}
\V 1
\begindata{text,44980829504}
\textdsversion{12}
[image]Shahrnush Parsipur, "the celebrated Iranian writer whose subversive 
works of feminist fiction saw her repeatedly imprisoned" under the Shah as 
well as the Islamic Republic, died July 3 at age 80, the 
\htmlatk-italic{\htmlatk-link{Guardian}} reported. Parsipur "excoriated the 
country's patriarchal culture" in novels like \htmlatk-italic{Women Without 
Men} and \htmlatk-italic{Touba and the Meaning of Night}. In 2026, a 
translation of \htmlatk-italic{Women Without Men} by Faridoun Farrokh was 
published in the U.K. and was longlisted for the International Booker 
Prize. \htmlatk-bold{\htmlatk-link{(continued)}}


\enddata{text,44980829504}
